--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Prior art this project builds on, and owes.

dagpane depends on none of the following. `dagpane-core`'s dependency list is one line,
serde. What follows is an intellectual debt ledger, and it is a real one — the ideas below
are most of this engine. Facts about each were verified 2026-09-08; COMPETITORS.md §5
carries the fuller version with download figures.

  salsa                            https://github.com/salsa-rs/salsa
    0.28.2, published 2026-08-03. Apache-2.0 OR MIT. Maintained by Lukas Wirth, who
    maintains rust-analyzer.

    **This is also "the rust-analyzer red-green algorithm". They are one codebase**, and
    listing them as two prior arts — which the brief this module came from did — is an
    error worth correcting here rather than repeating.

    Taken: **backdating** — stop propagating when a recomputed value equals the previous
    one. dagpane's digest comparison is that idea at cell granularity, and it is the single
    mechanism that makes an over-declared edge cost one recomputation instead of a cascade.
    Not taken: the demand-driven pull discipline and the global revision counter (dagpane
    pushes dirty marks over the structural closure and evaluates eagerly in ascending
    height), the compile-time query set (this graph is built at run time from a manifest),
    and durability tiers — which would be the right answer to "a 600-row CSV source should
    never be revalidated because a slider moved" and are not built.

  comemo                           https://github.com/typst/comemo
    0.5.1, published 2026-01-29. MIT OR Apache-2.0. Typst's memoization engine.

    Taken: nothing, in v0.1.0. It is recorded here because it is the idea this project most
    owes and has not paid. `#[track]` makes a type's *accesses* observable and `#[memoize]`
    reuses a cached result when only untouched parts of an argument changed; applied to a
    table that is column-level provenance — a cell reading 3 of 200 columns not
    invalidating when column 47 moves. dagpane invalidates at whole-value granularity:
    change one cell of a `Table` and everything reading that table recomputes.

  Shiny                            https://shiny.posit.co
    R, 2012. Posit. py-shiny 1.6.0 in 2026.

    **The origin of the reactive graph in a data-app framework**, and the credit belongs
    here rather than to any of the 2024–2026 arrivals. Also the demonstration that a
    reactive graph is worth *inspecting*: py-shiny 1.6.0's OpenTelemetry export is the
    ancestor of `dagpane explain`, and it inspects a running app where `explain` answers
    before a server exists. Not taken: read-time dependency capture, which is Shiny's actual
    mechanism — the reader is recorded when a value is read, so the graph is exact and needs
    no analysis. dagpane went the other way on purpose; docs/adr/0001 weighs the two and
    states the cost it accepts, which is an over-declared edge.

  Observable's runtime             https://github.com/observablehq/runtime
    Observable Framework 1.13.4, last npm publish 2026-03-02.

    Taken: the **topologically-sorted cell dataflow** shape. dagpane's ascending-height pass
    is the same family of answer to the same glitch problem — evaluate in an order where
    every input is final before its consumer runs. Not taken: Framework's build-time
    data-loader model, which computes snapshots once at build rather than per interaction.

  marimo                           https://github.com/marimo-team/marimo
    0.24.0, published 2026-08-17. Apache-2.0.

    The closest precedent, and it is not a prototype. marimo statically analyses each cell
    for references and definitions and reruns everything referencing what a cell defines,
    which is derivation dagpane does not attempt — a dagpane cell names its inputs and
    somebody types every edge. dagpane has no derivation claim against marimo and this
    project does not get to write one.

  Adapton
    The `adapton` crate: 0.3.31, last published 2019-12-22, MPL-2.0. Dead — it is cited here
    as papers, not as software, and depending on it would be a finding in any review.

    Taken: the papers. *Adapton: Composable, Demand-Driven Incremental Computation*
    (PLDI 2014) for the demanded computation graph, and *Incremental Computation with Names*
    (OOPSLA 2015) for **nominal matching** — naming allocations so a re-execution reuses the
    same node identity instead of allocating a fresh one. Not taken: the mechanism, which
    v0.1.0 does not need, because this graph is built once and never changes shape at run
    time, so node identity is trivially stable. The problem nominal Adapton names arrives
    the day a manifest can add or remove cells while a session is live.

**No dagpane code is derived from any of the prior art above.** The debt is to the designs,
and none of these projects has reviewed or endorsed this one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Written deliberately in-tree rather than taken from a dependency:

  the 128-bit FNV-1a digest        crates/core/src/digest.rs
    This is the one place in the project where a bug is silent — a digest that collides
    shows a user a stale number on a page that looks like it is working — and seventy-nine
    lines with the comments stripped, which a reviewer reads in full, are worth more here
    than a faster function they will not.
    `std::hash::Hasher` is also 64-bit and its integer encodings are implementation-defined,
    so a digest taken through it is a property of the platform rather than of the value.
    See docs/adr/0003.

  the CSV reader and its inference crates/app/src/csv.rs
    The inference is the work. A reader hands back strings; every column here has a type,
    and deciding it means a pass over the values anyway. Doing both in one pass is a hundred
    lines whose failure modes fit in a reviewer's head, against a dependency that would
    still leave the interesting half to write.

  the front end                    crates/serve/src/client.html
    One file, no build step, no package manager, nothing fetched at run time. A framework
    whose hello-world starts with `npm install` has a different first five minutes than one
    whose binary already contains the page, and the difference is most of the adoption. The
    cost is a hand-written renderer with two chart shapes and no charting library.

  the LCG in the differential test crates/core/tests/oracle.rs
    A failing seed has to be reproducible from the number printed in the assertion message,
    on any machine, for the life of the project. A generator whose sequence can change with
    a dependency bump cannot promise that.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Referenced, implemented from public specifications, not derived from any implementation:

  FNV-1a, 128-bit parameters       the reference specification's offset basis and prime,
                                   regrouped in fours so a reader can compare them digit by
                                   digit
  RFC 4180                         the CSV subset the reader accepts, and it accepts a
                                   subset: no alternative delimiters, no comment lines, no
                                   embedded newlines outside quotes, UTF-8 only
  Kahn's algorithm                 the build-time topological order and cycle detection

